      "text": "First, we need to determine the total number of books in the warehouse. Since there are 1335 boxes, each containing 39 books, the total number of books is:\n\n\\[ 1335 \\times 39 \\]\n\nWe calculate this product as follows:\n\n\\[ 1335 \\times 39 = 1335 \\times (40 - 1) = 1335 \\times 40 - 1335 \\times 1 = 53400 - 1335 = 52065 \\]\n\nSo, there are 52065 books in total. Next, we need to determine how many boxes Melvin can pack with 40 books each after packing as many boxes as possible. We do this by dividing the total number of books by 40:\n\n\\[ 52065 \\div 40 = 1301 \\] remainder \\( 25 \\)\n\nThis tells us that Melvin can pack 1301 boxes with 40 books each, and there will be \\( 52065 - 1301 \\times 40 = 52065 - 52040 = 25 \\) books left over. Therefore, the number of books left over is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{25} \\]",
